# plan spec

---
intent: >
  Stage 4 re-align of the `npx aidemd-mcp sync` verb. Two structural shifts:
  (1) Insert a null-bearing-args precondition at the boundary, BEFORE any
  `.mcp.json` mutation — when the post-interpolation `mcpServerConfig.args`
  contains JS `null` at any index, sync exits non-zero, names every
  offending index in stderr, routes the user to `/aide:brain config`, and
  leaves `.mcp.json` untouched. (2) Remove every trace of the legacy
  `mcpServers.obsidian` migration: the `entries["obsidian"] = "delete"`
  call, the `Removed legacy \`obsidian\` ...` stdout line, the file-level
  JSDoc reference, and the dedicated migration test (3c). The byte-faithful
  propagation contract for fully-wired configs, the idempotency contract,
  the existing exit-code map (0 success / 1 file failure / 2 help), the
  exclusive-mutation-surface invariant, and the read/merge/write helper
  call site stay unchanged in shape.
---

## Project Structure

This is an additive/edit plan against an existing module. The folder shape
is unchanged; only `index.ts` and `index.test.ts` are edited.

```
src/cli/sync/
├── .aide                        ← [existing — Stage 3 rewrite already in place]
├── plan.aide                    ← [this file — rewritten]
├── index.ts                     ← [edit] CLI entry point
│                                   exports:
│                                     export async function runSync(
│                                       cwd: string,
│                                       write?: (line: string) => void,
│                                       writeErr?: (line: string) => void,
│                                     ): Promise<number>
│                                   Pipeline (post-edit):
│                                     1. parseBrainAide(cwd) — branch on missing /
│                                        malformed-frontmatter / malformed-body, each
│                                        emits its own stderr line and returns 1.
│                                     2. Compute post-interpolation args:
│                                          args = interpolateArgs({ name, mcpServerConfig })
│                                        Type: (string | null)[]  (per parseBrainAide
│                                        prerequisite — see § Prerequisites).
│                                     3. NEW — Null-bearing-args precondition. Walk
│                                        `args` element-by-element with `=== null`
│                                        identity comparison, collecting every index
│                                        whose element is null. If any index is null,
│                                        emit a single stderr line naming every
│                                        offending index and routing to
│                                        `/aide:brain config`, return 1.
│                                        `.mcp.json` is NOT read or written on this
│                                        path.
│                                     4. Compose expectedEntry = { command, args }
│                                        (args are guaranteed string[] post-step-3).
│                                     5. writeMcpEntry(cwd, { brain: expectedEntry })
│                                        — REMOVED: the prior `obsidian: "delete"`
│                                        entry. The entries map now contains exactly
│                                        one key: `brain`.
│                                     6. Translate the helper's invalid-JSON throw to
│                                        a user-facing stderr line; return 1.
│                                     7. Report — Read stdout line, Wrote/already-in-
│                                        sync line, command/args dump, Done. The
│                                        `Removed legacy \`obsidian\` ...` line is
│                                        REMOVED.
│                                   Module-level JSDoc has the legacy-obsidian
│                                   sentence removed and the null-refusal precondition
│                                   added to the exit-code contract.
└── index.test.ts                ← [edit] co-located vitest suite
                                   - Test 3c (legacy obsidian migration) DELETED in
                                     full — including the describe block, helper
                                     pre-write of `mcpServers.obsidian`, and the
                                     migration-output assertion.
                                   - Test 3b (idempotent re-run) loses its inline
                                     comment about "obsidian absent" but keeps its
                                     pre-write of just the brain entry.
                                   - NEW test group 3l — null-bearing args refusal,
                                     covering: single-null exit-1 + stderr names
                                     index, multi-null stderr names every index in
                                     one line, .mcp.json is untouched after refusal
                                     (both byte-equal-after and absent-not-created
                                     subcases), null-refusal stderr routes to
                                     `/aide:brain config`.
                                   - All other test groups (3a, 3d–3k) keep their
                                     intent; only the EXPECTED_BRAIN_ENTRY shape and
                                     args fixtures stay aligned with the broadened
                                     `(string | null)[]` parser contract (no test
                                     edits required since the existing all-string
                                     fixtures remain valid input).
```

This plan does NOT touch:

- `src/cli/shared/writeMcpEntry/index.ts` — the helper takes an entries map
  and is schema-agnostic. No edit needed; the entries map this plan passes
  drops the `obsidian: "delete"` key but the helper handles a single-key
  map identically.
- `src/cli/shared/writeMcpEntry/index.test.ts` — helper tests still cover
  the `"delete"` sentinel; whether sync exercises it is sync's concern,
  not the helper's.
- `src/types/index.ts` — the `(string | null)[]` broadening lands inside
  the parseBrainAide plan (declared as a Prerequisite below). Sync
  consumes the broadened type via `ParseBrainAideResult`, never imports
  `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` directly.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` — the parser owns the
  `(string | null)[]` typing and `interpolateArgs`'s null-passthrough
  semantics. Sync calls them at the same call sites as today.

## Prerequisites

This plan depends on `src/service/parseBrainAide/` shipping the broadened
args type. The following must be true before this plan's steps execute:

**`src/service/parseBrainAide/`** must export:

- `parseBrainAide(root: string)` returning a `ParseBrainAideResult` whose
  `ok` variant carries `mcpServerConfig: { command: string; args: (string |
  null)[] }`. YAML null in the args sequence parses to JS `null` and is
  preserved verbatim through to the result — no coercion to empty string,
  no rejection as malformed-frontmatter.
- `interpolateArgs(config: BrainAideConfig): (string | null)[]` — walks the
  `args` array element-by-element, performs `${...}` substitution against
  top-level frontmatter fields on string entries, and passes null entries
  through unchanged. Return type matches the input element type.

**`src/types/index.ts`** must export `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` such that
`args: (string | null)[]`, with `BrainAideConfig` and the `kind: "ok"`
variant of `ParseBrainAideResult` consuming that broadened type.

If parseBrainAide is still on `args: string[]` when this plan runs:

- Step 2's null-detection step will type-error against `args.some((a) => a
  === null)` because TS will narrow the comparison to always-false on a
  `string[]`.
- The new test 3l fixtures will fail to parse (the parser will emit a
  malformed-frontmatter for "args contained a non-string element").

Run the parseBrainAide broadening plan first.

## Plan

### 1. Insert the null-bearing-args precondition into `runSync`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`,
`coding-playbook/services/error-handling`,
`coding-playbook/agentic/command-modules`

Per spec `outcomes.desired[2]` and `outcomes.desired[3]`: sync refuses
null-bearing args at the boundary. The check is a precondition — it does
not coerce, drop, or substitute — and it runs BEFORE any `.mcp.json`
read or write. Per spec Strategy paragraph "Null-refusal precondition at
the boundary, not transformation": the args array written to `.mcp.json`
is either the full post-interpolation array byte-for-byte (every slot a
string) or no write happens at all.

- [ ] 1a. Open `src/cli/sync/index.ts`. After the existing parseBrainAide
      result branching (the missing / malformed-frontmatter / malformed-body
      block) and after the existing line that computes the post-interpolation
      args via `interpolateArgs(...)`, insert a new precondition step. The
      step must execute BEFORE the `writeMcpEntry` call site; no `.mcp.json`
      I/O may occur on the null-refusal path.

      The precondition computes the list of null-valued indexes by walking
      `args` element-by-element and collecting every index `i` where
      `args[i] === null`. The check uses JS strict-identity `=== null` —
      not `typeof a === "object"`, not `!a`, not string equality against
      the literal `"null"`. Per the spec post-mortem on the retired
      `<BRAIN_PATH>` literal sentinel, the unwired-slot signal is JS null
      and only JS null. Treating any other value as the signal would
      reintroduce the literal-sentinel regression class.

- [ ] 1b. When the collected null-index list is non-empty, emit ONE stderr
      line and return 1. The line shape:

      `` `.aide/config/brain.aide` has unwired slots: args[<i>] is null<, args[<j>] is null...>. Run `/aide:brain config` to fill the unwired slot(s). ``

      Single-index case: `... args[3] is null. Run /aide:brain config ...`
      Multi-index case: `... args[3] is null, args[5] is null. Run
      /aide:brain config ...`

      Wording is fixed per the spec Strategy block "Failure messages are
      short, name the file, name the action" and the example in
      Good-examples ("Sync hard-fails on a null-bearing args array").
      The terminating sentence "Run `/aide:brain config` to fill the
      unwired slot(s)." is verbatim per the spec Strategy paragraph "The
      stderr message names the index explicitly and routes to
      /aide:brain config." When the offending count is one, "slot(s)"
      stays as "slot(s)" — the parenthetical handles both arities; do
      not branch the suffix on count.

- [ ] 1c. After the null-refusal precondition, the existing
      `expectedEntry` shape is still `{ command: ..., args: ... }`. Per the
      Prerequisites section, `interpolateArgs` returns `(string | null)[]`,
      so the `args` field on `expectedEntry` is still typed `(string |
      null)[]` from TypeScript's perspective at this point in the
      function. The null-refusal precondition has narrowed at the value
      level but not at the type level. Resolve via a type-narrowing
      pattern that preserves the precondition's load-bearing role:
      either a type-guard helper (`function isAllStrings(args: (string |
      null)[]): args is string[]`) called inside the precondition, or
      assign the precondition's filtered/checked output to a `string[]`
      variable derived from the `every`/index-walk result. Whichever
      pattern is chosen, the final `expectedEntry.args` value passed to
      `writeMcpEntry` MUST be typed `string[]` — the helper's `entries`
      map signature is `Record<string, McpServerEntry | "delete">` and
      `McpServerEntry.args: string[]`. Do not coerce the broadened type
      to `string[]` via `as string[]` cast; the precondition has done
      real runtime work and the type system should follow it.

      The contract this step preserves: from the spec
      `outcomes.desired[1]`: "computes the expected MCP entry as `{
      command: config.mcpServerConfig.command, args: interpolateArgs(config)
      }` and writes it into the host's `.mcp.json` under the fixed
      `brain` key, leaving every other key in `mcpServers` ... untouched."

- [ ] 1d. Confirm the existing `try/catch` around `writeMcpEntry` (which
      translates the helper's invalid-JSON throw to the action-naming
      stderr line) is unchanged. The null-refusal precondition lives
      BEFORE this try/catch and runs first; writeMcpEntry is never
      reached on the null-refusal path. Per the spec
      `outcomes.desired[2]` "`.mcp.json` is not touched on this path"
      — the precondition's return-1 short-circuits the entire write
      pipeline, including the read that `writeMcpEntry` performs to
      compute structural equality.

- [ ] 1e. Confirm the exit-code contract is preserved:
      - 0 on successful write OR no-change ("already in sync" remains
        success per spec `outcomes.desired[4]`).
      - 1 on missing brain.aide, malformed-frontmatter, malformed-body,
        null-bearing args (NEW source of exit-1 added by step 1b),
        malformed `.mcp.json`, any uncaught throw at the IIFE boundary.
      - 2 on `--help` or invalid argv (handled by the IIFE before
        runSync is called; unchanged).

      Per spec Strategy paragraph "Sync exits zero on success, non-zero
      on every other path" and "The null-refusal path uses exit code 1
      alongside the other brain.aide failure modes" — the precondition
      reuses exit code 1 because from the user's perspective it is the
      same action class: the source file isn't ready, fix it and re-run.

### 2. Remove the legacy `obsidian` migration code from `runSync`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`,
`coding-playbook/foundations/anti-patterns` (by reference — anti-pattern
is "code paths that exist only to handle dead schemas")

Per the architect instruction: the legacy `mcpServers.obsidian`
migration was dropped entirely from the design. No transitional path,
no fallback. Sync writes only `mcpServers.brain` and never inspects or
deletes any other key.

- [ ] 2a. In `src/cli/sync/index.ts`, locate the entries-map construction
      (currently lines ~65–68 in the existing file, the block that builds
      `Record<string, typeof expectedEntry | "delete">` with both `brain`
      and `obsidian` keys). Remove the `obsidian: "delete"` entry from
      the map. The map now carries exactly one key: `brain`.

      Also remove the inline comment that justified the unconditional
      delete ("Always set brain; always request obsidian deletion. The
      helper treats 'delete a key that isn't present' as a no-op, so
      including obsidian unconditionally is safe.") — the rationale is
      no longer relevant.

      The `Record` type annotation on `entries` still accepts a `"delete"`
      sentinel because the helper's signature does, but the map this
      file constructs no longer uses it. The type can stay as
      `Record<string, McpServerEntry>` (drop the `| "delete"` from the
      local annotation) since the call site no longer constructs delete
      sentinels. If keeping the broader type is more ergonomic for
      future-proofing, that's acceptable — the focus is removing the
      runtime `"delete"` value, not narrowing the type.

- [ ] 2b. In the report block (currently lines ~92–98), remove the
      conditional that fires the migration-message line:

      ```
      if (writeResult.deleted.includes("obsidian")) {
        write("Removed legacy `obsidian` MCP key (migrated to `brain`)");
      }
      ```

      Delete both the if-statement and its body. The `writeResult.deleted`
      field still exists on the helper's return shape but sync no longer
