### 3. Add the null-bearing-args refusal test group

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`,
`coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

Per spec `outcomes.desired[2]`, `outcomes.desired[3]`, and the
Good-examples block "Sync hard-fails on a null-bearing args array":
add a new describe block covering single-null, multi-null, exit code,
stderr message shape, and `.mcp.json` untouched-after-refusal.

- [ ] 3a. Open `src/cli/sync/index.test.ts`. Add a new describe block
      after test 3k (no prose interpolation), labeled `3l — null-bearing
      args refusal`. The block contains four `it` blocks, each
      independently verifying one facet of the precondition.

      Test 1 — "single null at args[3] exits 1 and stderr names the
      index": pre-write a brain.aide whose `mcpServerConfig.args`
      contains one YAML null at index 3 (the others are valid strings).
      The fixture must use the canonical six-section body schema (orientation
      + config + playbook-index + study-playbook + update-playbook +
      research-index marker pairs) so the parser does not reject it for
      malformed-body before the null-refusal precondition fires. The
      YAML representation of null-at-index-3 is a bare hyphen on its
      own line in the args sequence (e.g., `args:\n - 'a'\n - 'b'\n
      - 'c'\n -\n` — the trailing `-` followed by a newline parses to
      `null` per YAML 1.2).

      Assertions:
      - exit code === 1
      - stderr contains the substring `args[3] is null`
      - stderr contains the substring `/aide:brain config`
      - `.mcp.json` was NOT created (file does not exist post-call when
        not pre-written) OR is byte-identical to its pre-write state
        when the test pre-writes one — pick one subcase per `it` block.

      Test 2 — "multiple nulls at args[2] and args[4] are all named in
      stderr": pre-write a brain.aide with two YAML nulls at indexes 2
      and 4. After running sync, assert stderr contains BOTH
      `args[2] is null` and `args[4] is null` substrings, on the same
      line (the spec mandates a single stderr line naming all offending
      indexes per the Strategy paragraph "When multiple indexes are
      null, the message names all of them in one line"). Exit code 1.

      Test 3 — "null-refusal does not touch existing `.mcp.json`":
      pre-write a brain.aide with one null in args AND a pre-existing
      `.mcp.json` containing arbitrary `mcpServers` entries (e.g.,
      `mcpServers.aide` with an entry plus `mcpServers.brain` with
      stale-but-valid bytes from a prior sync). Read `.mcp.json` bytes
      before runSync; after runSync, read again; assert byte-equality.
      Per spec `outcomes.desired[2]` "`.mcp.json` is not touched on
      this path" — every key, including any prior `mcpServers.brain`
      entry from a previous successful sync, remains exactly as it
      was.

      Test 4 — "null-refusal does not coerce, drop, or substitute":
      pre-write a brain.aide with one null at args[3]. After runSync,
      open the brain.aide on disk and assert it is byte-identical to
      the pre-write state (the null is still null, not `""`, not the
      literal `"null"`, not a sentinel string). Sync must not have
      attempted to mutate brain.aide on the null-refusal path either —
      that is part of the spec's `undesired[7]` ("A sync verb that
      mutates anything under `.aide/config/`"). This test is the
      load-bearing structural assertion that the regression class
      retired in the parent spec's `<BRAIN_PATH>` post-mortem cannot
      reappear via sync's transformation surface.

- [ ] 3b. The test fixtures in 3l reuse the existing `writeBrainAide`
      and `writeMcpJson` helpers from the test file. The
      `makeCapture()` helper captures stdout and stderr per call so
      tests can assert the substring patterns above against `errLines`.

      Each test must use a fresh tempDir per `beforeEach` so the
      pre-write state is isolated (the existing `beforeEach`
      lifecycle handles this; no new lifecycle is needed).

      The fixtures' frontmatter `name` field can be any string — the
      tests do not branch on it. The args list before the null
      indexes can carry any byte-valid strings; the spec invariant
      tested is the null-detection mechanism, not the value of
      surrounding strings. Use the same `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`-style
      args used elsewhere in the file for consistency.

