## Decisions

- **Edit-not-rewrite scope.** The module already exists and works for
  fully-wired configs and the legacy-key migration. Stage 4's job is two
  surgical edits: insert a precondition, delete the legacy migration.
  The byte-faithful propagation contract, the idempotency contract, the
  exit-code map, the IIFE argv handling, and the read/merge/write helper
  call site are all unchanged in shape. Treating it as an edit (not a
  rewrite) keeps the diff reviewable and the risk surface small.

- **Precondition placement: after interpolation, before writeMcpEntry.**
  Per spec `outcomes.desired[2]` "if any element of `mcpServerConfig.args`
  is null at sync time (after interpolation), sync exits non-zero." The
  check runs against the `interpolateArgs(...)` output, not the raw
  parser output, because the substitution pass might (in some advanced-
  user configurations) replace a null entry with a string. Placing the
  check after interpolation is the spec-mandated order. Placing it
  before `writeMcpEntry` ensures `.mcp.json` is never read or written on
  the null-refusal path — per spec `outcomes.desired[2]` "`.mcp.json`
  is not touched on this path."

- **Strict `=== null` identity, not truthiness or string equality.** Per
  the spec post-mortem on the retired `<BRAIN_PATH>` literal sentinel:
  the unwired-slot signal is JS null and only JS null. Treating empty
  string, undefined, the literal `"null"`, or any other value as the
  signal would re-introduce the literal-sentinel regression class. The
  parser preserves YAML null as JS null; the precondition tests JS null
  with `=== null`.

- **Single stderr line names every null index.** Per spec Strategy
  paragraph "When multiple indexes are null, the message names all of
  them in one line." A single line is easier to grep, easier to
  cross-reference against the per-integration `config` body section
  (which names which arg indexes mean what), and matches the cold-start
  CLI's per-action log-line conventions.

- **Fixed terminating sentence "Run `/aide:brain config` to fill the
  unwired slot(s)."** Per spec Strategy paragraph "That phrasing matches
  the orchestrator routing the boot reporter uses for the
  `no-mcp-entry` brain status state, so the user sees the same recovery
  path from both surfaces." Reusing the verbatim phrase keeps the user-
  visible vocabulary consistent across stderr lines, boot reporter
  output, and the `/aide:brain config` command surface.

- **Null-refusal reuses exit code 1 alongside other brain.aide failures.**
  Per spec Strategy paragraph "The null-refusal path uses exit code 1
  alongside the other brain.aide failure modes, because from the user's
  perspective it's the same action class: the source file isn't ready,
  fix it and re-run." Introducing a new exit code for null-bearing args
  would split a single user-facing concept ("brain.aide isn't usable
  yet") into two terminal contracts. The orchestrator routing is
  already differentiated at the prose layer (the `no-mcp-entry` brain
  status state covers the null case); the CLI exit code stays unified.

- **Type narrowing via runtime guard, not `as string[]` cast.** Step
  1c calls for either a type-guard helper or an index-walk that
  produces a `string[]` typed value. Casting via `as string[]` would
  satisfy the type-checker without doing the runtime work; the guard
  pattern keeps the precondition's load-bearing status visible in the
  type system. Per the playbook entry
  `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions` § "Don't Guard Against
  Impossible States" — the type system follows the runtime check, not
  the other way around.

- **Legacy `obsidian` migration is removed entirely, not deprecated.**
  The architect instruction is unambiguous: "the legacy
  `mcpServers.obsidian` migration was dropped entirely (frontmatter and
  body both scrubbed)." Leaving the migration as a soft-deprecation
  (warn-but-don't-delete, or delete-but-don't-warn) is contradictory to
  the design. The entries map drops the `obsidian: "delete"` key
  entirely; the report no longer fires the migration line; the
  module-level JSDoc no longer documents the behavior. Future host
  projects upgrading from a pre-collapse version will retain their
  `mcpServers.obsidian` entry as inert configuration — sync neither
  reads nor mutates it. If hosts want it gone, they edit `.mcp.json`
  by hand (which is the visible-command-boundary stance the parent
  spec mandates anyway).

- **The matching test (3c) is deleted, not converted.** A converted
  test that asserts "obsidian is preserved" would be a new behavioral
  contract the spec does not request; sync's contract is "writes
  brain, leaves every other key untouched." That contract is already
  covered by test 3d ("preserve other keys"), which exercises the
  preservation invariant against `aide` and `custom-mcp` entries.
  Adding an `obsidian`-named entry to 3d would be redundant — sync
  treats every non-`brain` key identically (per the helper's merge
  semantics). The cleanest move is to delete 3c with no replacement.

- **Test numbering keeps the gap when 3c is removed.** Renumbering
  3d→3c, 3e→3d, etc. would touch every test header for a cosmetic
  shift. Section labels in this file are descriptive comments, not
  load-bearing identifiers; preserving the gap is the conservative
  diff. If the project's convention later mandates contiguous
  numbering, that's a separate cleanup pass.

- **Brain.aide is read-only on the null-refusal path.** Per spec
  `undesired[10]` "A sync verb that mutates anything under
  `.aide/config/`": sync may read brain.aide but never write to it,
  never patch it, never tidy its formatting, never fill a null slot
  directly. Test 3l's fourth assertion ("brain.aide is byte-identical
  after null refusal") is the structural lock against this regression.
  This is the same class of failure as the MCP server silently
  rewriting `.mcp.json`.

- **The `--help` and IIFE argv handling are unchanged.** Per spec
  `outcomes.desired[6]` "The CLI accepts no flags except `--help`."
  This stage adds no new flags, no new argv parsing, and no new
  behaviors visible to argv. The null-refusal precondition fires
  inside `runSync` after `parseBrainAide`; the IIFE's only job
  remains argv dispatch + exit-code propagation.

- **Path migrations remain in the Stage 3 plan; this stage does not
  re-touch them.** Stage 3 of this module's plan rewrote the path
  references from `.aide/brain.aide` to `.aide/config/brain.aide`
  and aligned the test fixtures to the two-field schema. Stage 4 (this
  plan) inherits that work; no further path or schema edits are
  needed in this pass. The diff Stage 4 produces is scoped to:
  inserting one precondition step, removing one map entry, removing
  one stdout line, removing one JSDoc sentence, adding one test
  group, removing one test group.


